<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Nothing in this app may leave the device by backup or by phone-to-phone
    transfer.

    android:allowBackup="false" is NOT sufficient on its own. Android's own
    documentation: for apps targeting API 31 or higher, "On devices from some
    device manufacturers, you can't disable device-to-device migration of your
    app's files." targetSdk here is 37, and the one test device is Samsung,
    which is squarely in that category.

    What would otherwise travel to a new handset:
      * the WebView localStorage: Maker API token, cloudSecret, both PIN
        hashes, camera URLs, and the Anthropic and Google API keys
      * hestia_hub_trust: the hub origin AND its pinned SPKI certificate hash
      * hestia_push: the FCM registration token

    The certificate pin is the sharp one. Carrying it to another device means
    the trust decision the user made on the trust screen is silently inherited
    by a handset they never made it on, which is the whole point of pinning
    undone by a migration wizard.

    So a Smart Switch style transfer would land a fully authorised Hestia on a
    new phone with no PIN, no re-pin and no confirmation. That is also the
    DESIRABLE behaviour for a legitimate upgrade, which is exactly why it has
    to be a decision rather than a default: setup is "install it and it finds
    your hub" with nothing typed, so re-pairing costs the user almost nothing,
    while a traded-in or resold phone carrying live household credentials costs
    them a great deal.

    Both sections are present and both are empty on purpose. The docs are
    explicit that silence is not denial: "If there are no rules for a
    particular backup mode, such as if the <device-transfer> section is
    missing, that mode is fully enabled for all content."
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude domain="root" />
        <exclude domain="file" />
        <exclude domain="database" />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" />
        <exclude domain="external" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude domain="root" />
        <exclude domain="file" />
        <exclude domain="database" />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" />
        <exclude domain="external" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
